From: Adriel Santos <adriel.turox@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] remoteproc: qcom_q6v5_mpd: fix user PD array overflow

qcom_get_pd_asid() derives the asid from the PD's spawn bit as bit / 8,
so on IPQ5018 it returns 1, 2 and 3 for pd-1, pd-2 and pd-3 - never 0.
q6_register_userpd() then stores each one at wcss->upd[pd_asid], but the
array only has MAX_UPD (3) entries, so registering pd-3 writes one
pointer past its end.

The array is currently the last member of struct q6_wcss, so the write
lands in slack space after the allocation and nothing visibly breaks.
It corrupts whatever is placed after it instead, which makes any later
addition to the structure fail in ways that have nothing to do with the
change that exposed it - found by adding a work_struct there, whose
->data the overflow clobbered so that schedule_work() silently did
nothing.

Size the array to hold asid MAX_UPD and reject an out-of-range asid
instead of scribbling past the end.

Signed-off-by: Adriel Santos <adriel.turox@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5_mpd.c
+++ b/drivers/remoteproc/qcom_q6v5_mpd.c
@@ -86,7 +86,11 @@
 	int num_clks;
 	const struct wcss_data *desc;
 	const char **firmware;
-	struct userpd *upd[MAX_UPD];
+	/*
+	 * Indexed by pd_asid, which qcom_get_pd_asid() derives from the PD's
+	 * spawn bit and which therefore runs 1..MAX_UPD, not 0..MAX_UPD-1.
+	 */
+	struct userpd *upd[MAX_UPD + 1];
 };
 
 struct userpd {
@@ -744,6 +748,13 @@
 	if (ret)
 		goto free_rproc;
 
+	if (upd->pd_asid >= ARRAY_SIZE(wcss->upd)) {
+		dev_err(wcss->dev, "%s: pd asid %u out of range\n",
+			userpd_np->name, upd->pd_asid);
+		ret = -ERANGE;
+		goto free_rproc;
+	}
+
 	wcss->upd[upd->pd_asid] = upd;
 	platform_set_drvdata(userpd_pdev, rproc);
 	qcom_add_ssr_subdev(rproc, &upd->ssr_subdev, userpd_pdev->name);
